WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.950 --> 00:00:08.440
My name is Wei and
I'm here together

00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:14.330
with Kiegan, who is down
there getting miked up and.

00:00:14.330 --> 00:00:17.770
Today, we're going to
talk to you about bees.

00:00:17.770 --> 00:00:22.810
So bees. We think R users have
a lot in common with bees.

00:00:22.810 --> 00:00:25.410
For one thing, we share
a love of hexagons.

00:00:28.110 --> 00:00:30.930
But more importantly,
bees are pollinators.

00:00:30.930 --> 00:00:33.930
And we know is that the
pollinators, the pollinator

00:00:33.930 --> 00:00:38.890
population, is really important
to the health of an ecosystem.

00:00:38.890 --> 00:00:42.660
And I just notice we have this
beautiful backdrop behind us

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:44.770
with these really
nice butterflies.

00:00:44.770 --> 00:00:49.330
So not we didn't plan this,
but it's kind of appropriate.

00:00:51.990 --> 00:00:56.610
And what we hope to convince
you of by the end of our talk is

00:00:56.610 --> 00:01:01.470
that like bees, R users can
enrich their own data ecosystem

00:01:01.470 --> 00:01:02.915
with effort and empathy.

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:08.920
So what do we mean
by data ecosystem?

00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:11.080
And to explain, I
think it's going

00:01:11.080 --> 00:01:13.810
to be helpful to tell you
a bit about ourselves.

00:01:13.810 --> 00:01:15.800
So Kiegan and I work at NORC.

00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:19.330
We are a nonpartisan social
research organization.

00:01:19.330 --> 00:01:23.180
And at NORC out coworkers,
our partners, our clients,

00:01:23.180 --> 00:01:26.600
we work on, we use
different types of software.

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:31.190
So SAS, Excel Stata.

00:01:31.190 --> 00:01:33.180
And we produce lots
of documentation.

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:37.310
So policy briefs, technical
reports and data code books,

00:01:37.310 --> 00:01:42.300
and we do that in a variety
of formats, like Word and PDF.

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:45.785
We work with expert coders, non
coders and everyone in between.

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:55.170
As R users and developers,
we have to leave the hive

00:01:55.170 --> 00:02:01.120
and we have to interact with all
these people in our ecosystem.

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:05.410
And to do so, I think
we're very lucky to have

00:02:05.410 --> 00:02:08.620
all these great tools that
folks in the R community

00:02:08.620 --> 00:02:13.260
have built to help to
facilitate these interactions.

00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:16.260
So so that's what we
mean by an ecosystem.

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:19.980
It's a diverse environment
where we have folks

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:24.090
with a range of skills using
different types of software

00:02:24.090 --> 00:02:28.220
and creating different
kinds of products.

00:02:28.220 --> 00:02:32.956
Why do we feel so strongly about
enriching this data ecosystem?

00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:40.700
Well, three reasons.

00:02:40.700 --> 00:02:45.920
So in our work, we either
succeed or we fail together.

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:50.680
So if our work is late
or we bust our budget,

00:02:50.680 --> 00:02:53.710
the client is not going to
care that the R team did

00:02:53.710 --> 00:02:55.510
this or SAS test
team didn't do that

00:02:55.510 --> 00:02:59.860
or many review took two
weeks longer than expected.

00:02:59.860 --> 00:03:03.910
So it's really important for
us to make sure that everybody

00:03:03.910 --> 00:03:11.630
succeeds, whether we are SAS
user or a Stata user or non coder.

00:03:11.630 --> 00:03:15.890
The second thing is,
well, as R users,

00:03:15.890 --> 00:03:18.740
we do better work when
others do better work.

00:03:18.740 --> 00:03:23.660
So it really helps us
when others thrive.

00:03:23.660 --> 00:03:27.560
And the third thing is
that the low the more we

00:03:27.560 --> 00:03:32.860
can lower the barrier to R for non
R users and beginner R users,

00:03:32.860 --> 00:03:35.980
and the more we can
show that R not only works

00:03:35.980 --> 00:03:40.630
with other software, but it
also works for other software,

00:03:40.630 --> 00:03:42.970
then it's easier for us
to convince our bosses

00:03:42.970 --> 00:03:46.750
to invest in the
infrastructure and to support

00:03:46.750 --> 00:03:48.820
R and other open
source software

00:03:48.820 --> 00:03:54.350
and also to pay for us to come
to this conference next year.

00:03:54.350 --> 00:03:58.336
So so that's why we care so much
about enriching the ecosystem.

00:04:01.540 --> 00:04:04.430
Before we dive into
how to be a pollinator.

00:04:04.430 --> 00:04:08.410
So just one word about
what we mean by empathy.

00:04:08.410 --> 00:04:10.540
When you when we
work in an ecosystem,

00:04:10.540 --> 00:04:13.040
you quickly realize that

00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:15.080
different people
have different ways

00:04:15.080 --> 00:04:20.600
of thinking about the world and
biologists have a word for it.

00:04:20.600 --> 00:04:23.540
It's called umwelt and
an umwelt is an animal's

00:04:23.540 --> 00:04:25.230
perceptual world.

00:04:25.230 --> 00:04:29.650
So every species senses the
world in a different way.

00:04:29.650 --> 00:04:34.740
So two species sharing the
same physical environment

00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:37.200
have completely
different experiences

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:38.290
of the same environment.

00:04:38.290 --> 00:04:42.510
So bats and whales, for example,
they hear the world in a world

00:04:42.510 --> 00:04:46.600
that human beings do not.

00:04:46.600 --> 00:04:48.170
Here is where empathy comes in.

00:04:48.170 --> 00:04:50.290
So among all the
species, humans are

00:04:50.290 --> 00:04:52.660
unique in that we
have a capacity

00:04:52.660 --> 00:04:56.020
to appreciate the
umwelt of other species.

00:04:56.020 --> 00:04:59.170
So in our context,
empathy means being

00:04:59.170 --> 00:05:02.230
able to perceive code and
perceive data in a way

00:05:02.230 --> 00:05:03.420
that other people do.

00:05:08.030 --> 00:05:10.940
Now again, I'm going
to share with you

00:05:10.940 --> 00:05:14.610
three lessons we've learned
on how to be a pollinator.

00:05:14.610 --> 00:05:17.180
And the first lesson
is do no harm.

00:05:17.180 --> 00:05:20.330
As R users and developers,
it's really important for us

00:05:20.330 --> 00:05:23.300
to be really careful and
intentional when we interact

00:05:23.300 --> 00:05:24.810
with others in our ecosystem

00:05:24.810 --> 00:05:29.590
and when we deal with
foreign data formats.

00:05:29.590 --> 00:05:35.350
So so here is one example of
reading data from Stata into R

00:05:35.350 --> 00:05:38.560
and they say that the lessons
that stick with you most

00:05:38.560 --> 00:05:40.310
are the ones you learn
from your mistakes.

00:05:40.310 --> 00:05:43.420
And so this is one that's
near and dear to my heart.

00:05:43.420 --> 00:05:45.790
Anyways, this example comes
from the General Social

00:05:45.790 --> 00:05:50.410
Survey about GSS and GSS
is one of the most widely cited

00:05:50.410 --> 00:05:53.020
studies of American society.

00:05:53.020 --> 00:05:58.050
It's a study that's been run
by NORC for the past 50 years.

00:05:58.050 --> 00:06:02.220
And here we have an entry
from the GSS codebook.

00:06:02.220 --> 00:06:03.930
This variable
describes the number

00:06:03.930 --> 00:06:07.530
of education received
by the respondent's mother.

00:06:07.530 --> 00:06:10.500
And the codebook tells
us what the unique values

00:06:10.500 --> 00:06:16.560
of this variable is and number
of cases with each value.

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:19.210
And then at the bottom, we have
this section reserved codes.

00:06:19.210 --> 00:06:21.220
Right? And these
are missing values.

00:06:21.220 --> 00:06:24.270
So there are three different
types of missing values.

00:06:24.270 --> 00:06:28.260
And we have 316 cases
that are missing value

00:06:28.260 --> 00:06:30.220
because they said
they don't know.

00:06:30.220 --> 00:06:33.480
And then we have 100 cases
that are missing values

00:06:33.480 --> 00:06:35.010
because they said
that they did not

00:06:35.010 --> 00:06:37.570
grow up with a female
caregiver growing up.

00:06:37.570 --> 00:06:40.330
So they were not
asked this question.

00:06:40.330 --> 00:06:42.340
Now when we read
this data, data file

00:06:42.340 --> 00:06:46.840
into R, we see that the
three different missing value

00:06:46.840 --> 00:06:50.840
types have been collapsed
into a single NA. Right?

00:06:50.840 --> 00:06:52.190
And this looks bad, right?

00:06:52.190 --> 00:06:54.580
Because it looks like we've
just lost information.

00:06:54.580 --> 00:06:56.800
So so what do we.

00:06:56.800 --> 00:07:01.360
Well, why this happens. If folks
are familiar with census data,

00:07:01.360 --> 00:07:04.360
is that whereas
these two software

00:07:04.360 --> 00:07:06.220
have extended
missing types so they

00:07:06.220 --> 00:07:09.520
can support multiple
missing value types, R only

00:07:09.520 --> 00:07:11.470
has a single system missing.

00:07:11.470 --> 00:07:14.890
Right so so what do what
do we do about this?

00:07:14.890 --> 00:07:19.180
Well, it turns out that the
R data object that we have,

00:07:19.180 --> 00:07:21.430
whether we've read in
the data using haven

00:07:21.430 --> 00:07:25.210
or we used the foreign package,
that R data object still

00:07:25.210 --> 00:07:27.610
contains these
missing value types.

00:07:27.610 --> 00:07:29.950
Right? And it takes a
little bit of effort

00:07:29.950 --> 00:07:35.080
and a few more lines of code to
recover the different types.

00:07:35.080 --> 00:07:38.590
Folks who are interested can see
haven's conversion semantics vignette.

00:07:38.590 --> 00:07:40.420
for more information.
But our point

00:07:40.420 --> 00:07:44.380
here is that when you're working
with a new or foreign data

00:07:44.380 --> 00:07:47.740
format, it really is
important to take a moment

00:07:47.740 --> 00:07:49.480
to learn about
the idiosyncrasies

00:07:49.480 --> 00:07:52.000
of that specific format.

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:53.415
And it's part of doing no harm.

00:07:55.990 --> 00:07:58.090
Here is another example.

00:07:58.090 --> 00:08:00.700
Here we have a screenshot
of an Excel spreadsheet.

00:08:00.700 --> 00:08:02.560
The contents don't
really matter,

00:08:02.560 --> 00:08:05.770
but you can see that a few
rows have been highlighted

00:08:05.770 --> 00:08:09.940
and so there is data
in the formatting.

00:08:09.940 --> 00:08:12.460
And these could be
cases that have been

00:08:12.460 --> 00:08:15.310
flagged by a team for review.

00:08:15.310 --> 00:08:21.820
So some people have feelings
about formatting as data,

00:08:21.820 --> 00:08:28.000
but our view is that this
data can be important,

00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:30.080
and it's important that
we preserve this data.

00:08:30.080 --> 00:08:33.520
The great thing about R
is that these packages,

00:08:33.520 --> 00:08:36.190
openxlsx, tidyxl
that really helps

00:08:36.190 --> 00:08:40.950
us to retain this information
in a very easy way.

00:08:40.950 --> 00:08:44.100
And we should take the
effort a few lines of code

00:08:44.100 --> 00:08:47.230
to preserve this information.

00:08:47.230 --> 00:08:50.320
So the lesson here is it's
going to take a bit of effort

00:08:50.320 --> 00:08:52.090
and a bit of empathy.

00:08:52.090 --> 00:08:53.800
But it's really
important as pollinators

00:08:53.800 --> 00:08:55.840
that we take care with
other people's data

00:08:55.840 --> 00:08:58.420
and we do no harm.

00:08:58.420 --> 00:09:01.000
So I'm going to turn
it over to Kiegan.

00:09:01.000 --> 00:09:01.750
All right.

00:09:01.750 --> 00:09:03.180
Can can you hear me?

00:09:03.180 --> 00:09:03.730
Is my mic on?

00:09:03.730 --> 00:09:04.990
Awesome. Thank you.

00:09:04.990 --> 00:09:07.710
So the second principle
of how to be a pollinator

00:09:07.710 --> 00:09:09.740
is to make things
that just work.

00:09:09.740 --> 00:09:11.520
So what we really
mean by this is

00:09:11.520 --> 00:09:14.520
we want to remove barriers
for either people interacting

00:09:14.520 --> 00:09:16.620
with R or people
who are using things

00:09:16.620 --> 00:09:18.810
that we're creating
in R to make it

00:09:18.810 --> 00:09:21.620
so that things
just work for them.

00:09:21.620 --> 00:09:25.760
So a good example of making
things that just work is

00:09:25.760 --> 00:09:28.470
consider how your data
are being outputted.

00:09:28.470 --> 00:09:31.712
So you might take the
usual approach of I'm

00:09:31.712 --> 00:09:34.170
going to share this data that
I've cleaned and wrangled in

00:09:34.170 --> 00:09:35.760
R. It's really awesome looking.

00:09:35.760 --> 00:09:36.890
It's tidy.

00:09:36.890 --> 00:09:39.290
I'm going to save
it out as a CSV.

00:09:39.290 --> 00:09:41.000
So that's one approach.

00:09:41.000 --> 00:09:43.790
If you think about who the
next user is going to be,

00:09:43.790 --> 00:09:45.630
you could also take
a different approach.

00:09:45.630 --> 00:09:48.650
And again, with one
line of code here,

00:09:48.650 --> 00:09:51.320
using the foreign package,
we can write foreign

00:09:51.320 --> 00:09:55.580
and include the command
package equals SAS

00:09:55.580 --> 00:09:57.690
and that gives us a SAS script.

00:09:57.690 --> 00:10:01.760
So now not only do we have the
CSV, but the next person who

00:10:01.760 --> 00:10:04.250
uses this data, if they're
going to use it in SAS,

00:10:04.250 --> 00:10:06.410
they have a script
that reads in the data

00:10:06.410 --> 00:10:09.110
and adds the correct
formatting and labels for them

00:10:09.110 --> 00:10:10.430
automatically.

00:10:10.430 --> 00:10:13.910
So this is a really
great way to make sure

00:10:13.910 --> 00:10:16.250
that the next person
has an easier time.

00:10:16.250 --> 00:10:19.740
They go ahead and run this
script and the data just works.

00:10:19.740 --> 00:10:22.430
They don't have to go
digging for the formatting

00:10:22.430 --> 00:10:24.320
and the labels and
reencode everything

00:10:24.320 --> 00:10:26.100
in a way that
makes sense to SAS.

00:10:26.100 --> 00:10:27.000
It just works.

00:10:27.000 --> 00:10:27.805
It's already there.

00:10:30.400 --> 00:10:35.320
Another example is something
you've maybe run into a message

00:10:35.320 --> 00:10:37.240
like, this looks like
there's a hiccup.

00:10:37.240 --> 00:10:38.950
The server hosted
dashboard is not

00:10:38.950 --> 00:10:40.510
filtering based on
the uploaded file,

00:10:40.510 --> 00:10:42.650
but it's working on
my local computer.

00:10:42.650 --> 00:10:46.580
So maybe you've gotten this
message, this panicked email.

00:10:46.580 --> 00:10:49.420
Maybe you sent this panicked
email at some point.

00:10:49.420 --> 00:10:51.280
It's working on one
computer, but it's not

00:10:51.280 --> 00:10:52.095
working on another.

00:10:52.095 --> 00:10:54.470
It's working on the server,
but it's not working locally.

00:10:54.470 --> 00:10:57.850
So for somebody who's not
super confident in figuring out

00:10:57.850 --> 00:11:01.350
what errors are going wrong,
this can be really scary.

00:11:01.350 --> 00:11:03.100
This can be a way that
they're interacting

00:11:03.100 --> 00:11:05.680
with R that they don't
know what is going wrong

00:11:05.680 --> 00:11:07.400
and how to fix it.

00:11:07.400 --> 00:11:10.060
So we can remove
this possible barrier

00:11:10.060 --> 00:11:12.712
by, from the start, using
something like renv.

00:11:12.712 --> 00:11:14.420
And there are a lot
of different options.

00:11:14.420 --> 00:11:16.810
But essentially if you
don't know what renv is,

00:11:16.810 --> 00:11:18.430
go look it up.

00:11:18.430 --> 00:11:21.280
But it allows us to keep
track of the package versions

00:11:21.280 --> 00:11:24.550
we're using when we're
writing and implementing code

00:11:24.550 --> 00:11:28.210
and share those with any
other, any other people

00:11:28.210 --> 00:11:31.340
or environments that we're
going to use this code on.

00:11:31.340 --> 00:11:33.400
So the next time we
go to run this code,

00:11:33.400 --> 00:11:35.440
it works on the server
and it works locally,

00:11:35.440 --> 00:11:37.850
it works on your machine
and it works on my machine.

00:11:37.850 --> 00:11:40.030
And when we come back to
this code in six months,

00:11:40.030 --> 00:11:42.260
it runs exactly like
it's supposed to.

00:11:42.260 --> 00:11:45.040
It just works.

00:11:45.040 --> 00:11:47.800
So again, with a
little bit of effort

00:11:47.800 --> 00:11:50.980
and a bit of empathy for
how other people are going

00:11:50.980 --> 00:11:54.307
to be interacting with the
things you are creating in R,

00:11:54.307 --> 00:11:56.140
you can build a positive
interaction, right?

00:11:56.140 --> 00:11:58.600
A positive interaction
with R or something

00:11:58.600 --> 00:12:01.780
you've created in R can kind
of smooth out those friction

00:12:01.780 --> 00:12:04.150
points that people run
into and make things

00:12:04.150 --> 00:12:07.600
that just work for people.

00:12:07.600 --> 00:12:10.490
So the third principle
is to promote growth.

00:12:10.490 --> 00:12:14.260
And we really want to bring as
many people into the R fold

00:12:14.260 --> 00:12:17.570
and into the R community at
our organization as possible.

00:12:17.570 --> 00:12:20.560
And so we do this
in a couple of ways.

00:12:20.560 --> 00:12:22.600
The first that we
do pretty often

00:12:22.600 --> 00:12:25.570
is have newer R users interact
with existing scripts

00:12:25.570 --> 00:12:26.660
and processes.

00:12:26.660 --> 00:12:27.830
So maybe a more advanced.

00:12:27.830 --> 00:12:32.350
R user is going to write a
script or some sort of process

00:12:32.350 --> 00:12:34.100
to complete a task.

00:12:34.100 --> 00:12:36.580
Maybe it's a data
analytics, data analysis,

00:12:36.580 --> 00:12:39.820
maybe it creates a table,
maybe it creates a chart,

00:12:39.820 --> 00:12:43.270
and we can have newer users
take that already written code

00:12:43.270 --> 00:12:46.300
and run it with maybe
changes to certain functions,

00:12:46.300 --> 00:12:50.560
certain parameters or
flags within that code.

00:12:50.560 --> 00:12:54.070
And this may lead to
the following scenario.

00:12:54.070 --> 00:12:56.920
You're trying to figure out what
exactly happened to the script

00:12:56.920 --> 00:12:57.890
that you wrote.

00:12:57.890 --> 00:13:00.850
So you ran lines 1
through 57, then you

00:13:00.850 --> 00:13:05.360
changed overwrite to true, then
61 through 66, then back to 37.

00:13:05.360 --> 00:13:08.650
And this can be a really
frustrating conversation

00:13:08.650 --> 00:13:11.740
to have if for both
people, if you're

00:13:11.740 --> 00:13:14.080
trying to figure out
exactly what happened

00:13:14.080 --> 00:13:15.590
and exactly what went wrong.

00:13:15.590 --> 00:13:17.740
And now the person interacting
with this code who's

00:13:17.740 --> 00:13:19.690
maybe a newer R user
is going to feel

00:13:19.690 --> 00:13:22.150
frustrated and a little scared.

00:13:22.150 --> 00:13:24.610
So there's a couple of
ways that you can just

00:13:24.610 --> 00:13:26.300
make this a little bit easier.

00:13:26.300 --> 00:13:29.800
And provide a more
low risk way for them

00:13:29.800 --> 00:13:32.860
to interact with this code.

00:13:32.860 --> 00:13:36.910
So one is you can use a dialog
box, so something like the

00:13:36.910 --> 00:13:41.410
svDialogs package, you can
write again one line of code

00:13:41.410 --> 00:13:44.840
and you can have a user
go run an R script.

00:13:44.840 --> 00:13:46.420
And when it gets
to a certain point

00:13:46.420 --> 00:13:48.910
where you've inserted
this dialog box code,

00:13:48.910 --> 00:13:51.190
a dialog box will
pop up and give them

00:13:51.190 --> 00:13:53.000
the option to select Yes or No.

00:13:53.000 --> 00:13:55.090
So maybe it's whether
you're going to include

00:13:55.090 --> 00:13:56.567
a certain set of NAs or not.

00:13:56.567 --> 00:13:58.150
Maybe it's whether
you're going to run

00:13:58.150 --> 00:14:02.710
this for males or females,
whatever it is that it's

00:14:02.710 --> 00:14:05.188
a much easier way for them
to interact with the code,

00:14:05.188 --> 00:14:06.730
and it's much less
likely that you're

00:14:06.730 --> 00:14:09.550
going to run into this tracking
down what lines of code

00:14:09.550 --> 00:14:12.670
got run, at what
time and what order.

00:14:12.670 --> 00:14:14.290
So with a little
more effort, you

00:14:14.290 --> 00:14:17.380
can also give them a more
user friendly interface

00:14:17.380 --> 00:14:18.320
to interact with.

00:14:18.320 --> 00:14:22.150
So here's an example
of a process where

00:14:22.150 --> 00:14:27.310
we have analysts run dozens of
reports over a couple of months

00:14:27.310 --> 00:14:30.670
each year, and we built a
Shiny dashboard for them.

00:14:30.670 --> 00:14:35.030
So they can see what
reports need to be run.

00:14:35.030 --> 00:14:37.190
There's some of this
metadata here redacted.

00:14:37.190 --> 00:14:39.678
But how many reports
are left to run?

00:14:39.678 --> 00:14:40.970
How many have already been run?

00:14:40.970 --> 00:14:43.420
And they can select a
specific report in the table

00:14:43.420 --> 00:14:45.280
and go to the details
of that report,

00:14:45.280 --> 00:14:47.410
see all the metadata
that they need,

00:14:47.410 --> 00:14:49.390
and see all these
action buttons that

00:14:49.390 --> 00:14:51.970
let them take certain
steps in the process.

00:14:51.970 --> 00:14:57.790
So the users are interacting
with buttons in this

00:14:57.790 --> 00:15:01.780
without actually having
to run the code themselves

00:15:01.780 --> 00:15:06.140
and they're able to see
exactly the products of this.

00:15:06.140 --> 00:15:09.100
These beautiful created
R Markdown documents

00:15:09.100 --> 00:15:12.140
without having to run the code
themselves or dig into it.

00:15:12.140 --> 00:15:15.220
So a good example of how
this can promote growth

00:15:15.220 --> 00:15:17.530
is we had an analyst
start and say, hey,

00:15:17.530 --> 00:15:19.280
I'm honestly not
sure what happened.

00:15:19.280 --> 00:15:20.210
I caused this error.

00:15:20.210 --> 00:15:22.270
I thought I did
everything right.

00:15:22.270 --> 00:15:25.630
So I dug into it, figured
out the error fix,

00:15:25.630 --> 00:15:28.480
fixed it and got back
to them and they said,

00:15:28.480 --> 00:15:30.800
I'd really be interested
in what went wrong.

00:15:30.800 --> 00:15:33.010
I really want to get
better at using R. So

00:15:33.010 --> 00:15:35.290
I explained the process
and what went wrong

00:15:35.290 --> 00:15:36.230
and why it was fixed now.

00:15:36.230 --> 00:15:39.200
Cut to a few weeks later.

00:15:39.200 --> 00:15:41.620
And I get the following message
after they hit an error.

00:15:41.620 --> 00:15:43.340
I'm looking at
the log right now.

00:15:43.340 --> 00:15:45.220
So far I only see
warnings, but I'm

00:15:45.220 --> 00:15:46.660
going to go look for the error.

00:15:46.660 --> 00:15:49.120
So now, within the
span of about a month,

00:15:49.120 --> 00:15:54.070
this person went from not having
any idea how to figure out

00:15:54.070 --> 00:15:56.740
what caused the error to
digging into the logs themselves

00:15:56.740 --> 00:15:58.820
and trying to figure
out what the error was.

00:15:58.820 --> 00:16:00.940
And really kind of
learning on their own

00:16:00.940 --> 00:16:03.670
and trying to get better.

00:16:03.670 --> 00:16:07.270
So with a little bit of empathy
and a little bit more effort,

00:16:07.270 --> 00:16:09.370
maybe building out a
whole user interface,

00:16:09.370 --> 00:16:13.240
we've provided people a learning
opportunity and a low risk

00:16:13.240 --> 00:16:16.630
environment to see how powerful
R code can be and see

00:16:16.630 --> 00:16:21.340
how it runs without having
to open the door to maybe

00:16:21.340 --> 00:16:23.930
scary mistakes that they're
not sure how to fix.

00:16:23.930 --> 00:16:26.410
So we can promote
growth in users who may

00:16:26.410 --> 00:16:29.310
be interested in learning more.

00:16:29.310 --> 00:16:30.990
So what we want to
leave you with today

00:16:30.990 --> 00:16:32.520
is to think about
the question, how

00:16:32.520 --> 00:16:36.210
can you pollinate your own
data workflows using R?

00:16:36.210 --> 00:16:38.280
So can you do no harm?

00:16:38.280 --> 00:16:41.130
Can you make things that
just work and can you

00:16:41.130 --> 00:16:42.280
promote growth?

00:16:42.280 --> 00:16:47.800
And I would posit that
you can, but thank you.

00:16:47.800 --> 00:16:49.090
Thank you.

00:16:49.090 --> 00:16:50.590
But think about how
you can be this

00:16:50.590 --> 00:16:51.130
bee, right?

00:16:51.130 --> 00:16:53.130
Think about how you can travel
between different environments

00:16:53.130 --> 00:16:54.580
and work with different people.

00:16:54.580 --> 00:16:58.710
And think about what you can
learn from having empathy

00:16:58.710 --> 00:17:00.960
from the perspective
of others and think

00:17:00.960 --> 00:17:05.579
about what positive impact
you can leave behind with you.

00:17:05.579 --> 00:17:08.210
So thank you.

